00:11:54.240 --> 00:11:58.557
Part one, you did something that many,
many people do, which is you were quite

00:11:58.640 --> 00:12:03.237
nervous in the beginning, and that
affected your fluency a little bit.

00:12:03.320 --> 00:12:05.840
Myself, as a native English speaker,
if I was doing this test,

00:12:05.923 --> 00:12:09.957
the first few answers probably wouldn't be
the most fluent ones because you are

00:12:10.040 --> 00:12:11.320
a little nervous in the beginning.

00:12:11.403 --> 00:12:15.600
That is why in part one, they don't start
off with the really difficult questions.

00:12:15.840 --> 00:12:20.237
They just throw a little getting
to know you questions at you.

00:12:20.320 --> 00:12:22.400
But you coped pretty well after that.

00:12:22.520 --> 00:12:28.280
So as you went on, your answers became
more fluent and you became less nervous.

00:12:28.680 --> 00:12:31.040
And a good examiner will recognize that.

00:12:31.320 --> 00:12:35.597
A lot of people think that if you have one
bad answer, that you

00:12:35.680 --> 00:12:36.880
immediately get a low score.

00:12:36.963 --> 00:12:38.400
That's not how the test works.

00:12:38.640 --> 00:12:43.717
The examiner will base your score
on the totality of your answers.

00:12:43.800 --> 00:12:47.240
For example, you gave some very short
answers at the beginning, or you just were

00:12:47.560 --> 00:12:49.477
like, oh, listed lots of different things.

00:12:49.560 --> 00:12:53.480
The examiner knows that's just nerves
based on your subsequent answers.

00:12:53.600 --> 00:12:58.720
So pick the things that are easy to talk
about, and it's okay to have some nerves.

00:12:58.920 --> 00:13:00.920
Just understand that that's part of the test.

00:13:01.280 --> 00:13:05.037
Part two, a similar thing at the beginning
of part two, you could tell

00:13:05.120 --> 00:13:06.720
that you were thinking a lot.

00:13:06.960 --> 00:13:10.880
You were trying to come up
with the best ideas, I think.

00:13:11.120 --> 00:13:13.437
Is that true or not?
Yes.

00:13:13.520 --> 00:13:15.760
So remember that it's not an ideas test.

00:13:16.120 --> 00:13:17.160
It is a speaking test.

00:13:17.520 --> 00:13:21.357
A lot of people think that they'll get
a higher score or sound more impressive

00:13:21.440 --> 00:13:23.280
if they choose the best ideas.

00:13:23.400 --> 00:13:26.837
The best ideas are the ones that are
easiest for you to talk about,

00:13:26.920 --> 00:13:27.880
the simplest ideas.

00:13:28.160 --> 00:13:32.320
At the beginning, your fluency, it wasn't
bad or anything like that,

00:13:32.560 --> 00:13:34.126
but it's just that you were,

00:13:34.326 --> 00:13:36.997
you could tell that the cogs in your brain were working

00:13:37.080 --> 00:13:39.664
overtime trying to think
of the correct thing,

00:13:39.864 --> 00:13:42.360
and our brains are obviously
connected to our speech.

00:13:42.920 --> 00:13:46.517
And if our brains are overworked,
just like a computer with too many tabs

00:13:46.600 --> 00:13:50.543
open, then the speech
coming out will be affected sometimes.

00:13:50.743 --> 00:13:52.120
It wasn't that affected,

00:13:52.240 --> 00:13:55.597
but again, the examiner won't listen
to the first few sentences

00:13:55.680 --> 00:13:57.477
and go, terrible fluency.

00:13:57.560 --> 00:14:00.317
Not that it was terrible,
but a lot of people watching

00:14:00.400 --> 00:14:01.560
think that that's the case.

00:14:02.000 --> 00:14:06.677
They've been taught in their school
that if they don't sound fluent the entire

00:14:06.760 --> 00:14:08.720
time, that they're going
to get a lower score.

00:14:08.803 --> 00:14:12.517
That is not the case.
But overall, your part two was great.

00:14:12.600 --> 00:14:14.597
Some great vocabulary, some great grammar.

00:14:14.680 --> 00:14:20.197
But on test day, you might get a question
that you don't feel comfortable talking

00:14:20.280 --> 00:14:23.640
about or is just unusual for you.

00:14:23.760 --> 00:14:29.437
That could affect your fluency because
unusual topic plus overthinking could

00:14:29.520 --> 00:14:31.800
lead to you just having fluency issues.

00:14:32.080 --> 00:14:35.517
So again, my advice is pretty repetitive,
but talk about the things

00:14:35.600 --> 00:14:36.720
that are easy to talk about.

00:14:36.920 --> 00:14:39.917
Know that it's not a performance,
it's a conversation.

00:14:40.000 --> 00:14:40.160
All right?

00:14:40.243 --> 00:14:44.717
So sometimes it felt like you were
performing rather than talking to me.

00:14:44.800 --> 00:14:45.640
Everybody is different.

00:14:45.723 --> 00:14:48.877
Some people don't like making eye contact,
but when you were thinking,

00:14:48.960 --> 00:14:50.637
you were looking away and looking up.

00:14:50.720 --> 00:14:52.800
A lot of students do that.
They always look up.

00:14:52.960 --> 00:14:55.997
Maybe that's part of your brain that deals
with ideas, and you can tell

00:14:56.080 --> 00:14:57.080
they're really thinking deeply.

00:14:57.163 --> 00:15:02.760
When you did that, you were thinking
a lot and your fluency suffered at times.

00:15:02.920 --> 00:15:04.357
You did this thing as well.

00:15:04.440 --> 00:15:08.040
You did it at the start of part two
and you did it in part three,

00:15:08.560 --> 00:15:13.560
where you think and speak at the same
time and you usually repeat the question.

00:15:13.800 --> 00:15:18.237
So what you do is you're like,
so describe a time when you had

00:15:18.320 --> 00:15:21.080
to adapt to a new situation.

00:15:21.880 --> 00:15:25.920
So you're talking and thinking all at
the same time and repeating the question.

00:15:26.160 --> 00:15:30.317
A better way might be to just give
yourself a little bit more time.

00:15:30.400 --> 00:15:32.480
You don't have to answer immediately.

00:15:32.760 --> 00:15:36.400
Think about it for a second or two and
just say, that's an interesting question.

00:15:36.483 --> 00:15:40.317
I haven't thought about that one before,
or that's a really good question.

00:15:40.400 --> 00:15:44.280
Let me think about that for a second
and then give your answer.

00:15:44.480 --> 00:15:48.117
Because some examiners might think
that you're just repeating

00:15:48.200 --> 00:15:49.560
the question back at them.

00:15:49.800 --> 00:15:53.557
So that's not really showing how great
your English is, and your fluency

00:15:53.640 --> 00:15:55.300
is not great at the same time.

00:15:55.560 --> 00:16:00.837
The first 10, 20% of your answers,
the fluency seems to be lower than

00:16:00.920 --> 00:16:05.040
the final 80, 90% because
you're thinking what to say.

00:16:05.240 --> 00:16:06.520
Yes.
Does that make sense?

00:16:07.040 --> 00:16:08.160
Yes.
Is that helpful?

00:16:08.480 --> 00:16:11.237
You probably don't even
know you're doing it.

00:16:11.320 --> 00:16:12.560
It's probably just a habit.

00:16:12.760 --> 00:16:15.917
And in normal life,
you probably don't need to do that,

00:16:16.000 --> 00:16:18.720
but we want to make sure that you
get one of the top marks.

00:16:19.360 --> 00:16:23.560
Another way that you could improve,
especially your part three,

00:16:23.840 --> 00:16:25.800
would be to include more examples.

00:16:26.280 --> 00:16:31.317
So you talk generally about situations
that happen, like when you were crying

00:16:31.400 --> 00:16:34.240
in college or the values
that you disagreed with.

00:16:34.560 --> 00:16:36.640
Maybe you didn't want to say that because
you don't want to be

00:16:36.723 --> 00:16:38.397
controversial or whatever.

00:16:38.480 --> 00:16:40.840
Maybe you want to not offend anyone.

00:16:41.040 --> 00:16:43.517
But in the real test,
the cameras are not rolling.

00:16:43.600 --> 00:16:47.997
Feel free to give those real
examples because they're

00:16:48.080 --> 00:16:49.600
often easier to talk about.

00:16:50.000 --> 00:16:54.397
Another way is just to imagine you're
talking to someone who doesn't know

00:16:54.480 --> 00:16:57.680
anything about what you're talking about
and you need to help them understand.

00:16:57.880 --> 00:17:02.277
So using examples is just
a way that human beings help,

00:17:02.360 --> 00:17:03.720
it's just a communications tool.

00:17:03.803 --> 00:17:05.780
Explanations are communications tools.

00:17:06.040 --> 00:17:10.560
Again, nothing bad, but we want to make
sure that you get one of the top marks,

00:17:10.760 --> 00:17:15.237
and these are just
me being overly cautious and helping

00:17:15.320 --> 00:17:16.720
the people learn at home as well.

00:17:17.120 --> 00:17:18.397
So what scores would you get?

00:17:18.480 --> 00:17:20.877
The best part of your performance
is your pronunciation.

00:17:20.960 --> 00:17:23.600
Even though you didn't grow up
in an English-speaking country,

00:17:23.683 --> 00:17:25.477
you have picked up the accent.

00:17:25.560 --> 00:17:28.957
You don't sound like an American per se,
but you don't sound like you're

00:17:29.040 --> 00:17:31.480
from Lebanon or Iraq either.

00:17:31.600 --> 00:17:33.680
Now, you don't get extra marks
for sounding American,

00:17:33.763 --> 00:17:38.800
but you do get extra marks
for being as clear as possible.

00:17:39.160 --> 00:17:42.877
And the thing about the American accent,
not a lot of people know this,

00:17:42.960 --> 00:17:47.957
but the reason why the American accent is
generally clear is because so many people

00:17:48.040 --> 00:17:50.717
from so many different parts
of the world move to America.

00:17:50.800 --> 00:17:54.237
So everybody developed this
accent that is super clear.

00:17:54.320 --> 00:17:57.677
So it's actually a good accent
to have for communication.

00:17:57.760 --> 00:18:00.037
Standard American accent.
Standard American accent.

00:18:00.120 --> 00:18:02.760
Most people in the world will
be able to understand you.

00:18:03.080 --> 00:18:07.480
Whereas me, I'm Irish,
not everyone understands the Irish accent.

00:18:07.920 --> 00:18:13.437
So I'm not saying that anybody watching
needs to move to America or learn

00:18:13.520 --> 00:18:18.640
the American accent, but having that
accent for you, it doesn't sound forced.

00:18:18.800 --> 00:18:20.600
It doesn't sound like
you're putting it on.

00:18:20.683 --> 00:18:24.840
It sounds like that's your natural accent,
and it really does help communication.

00:18:25.000 --> 00:18:29.400
The other thing that spending time
in America has helped you do is to sound

00:18:29.680 --> 00:18:32.720
more like a native English speaker
with things like intonation,

00:18:32.803 --> 00:18:35.000
connected speech, word stress.

00:18:35.240 --> 00:18:39.800
This is where we show meaning through,
for example, our voice is going up

00:18:40.480 --> 00:18:41.560
or our voice is going down,

00:18:41.643 --> 00:18:47.289
like for example, when we're listing things,
I like sushi and I like Italian food

00:18:47.489 --> 00:18:51.360
and I like steak, and I also like Indian food.

00:18:51.680 --> 00:18:55.160
So I went up and I went up and I went up,
and then I went down at the end.

00:18:55.360 --> 00:18:58.117
I'm listing, I'm listing, I'm listing, now I'm done.

00:18:58.200 --> 00:19:03.720
So I'm conveying meaning through tone,
not through actual words.

00:19:04.000 --> 00:19:05.520
And you do that very, very well.

00:19:05.800 --> 00:19:09.360
So Band 9 for pronunciation.
Very, very good.

00:19:09.520 --> 00:19:11.597
You also have amazing vocabulary.

00:19:11.680 --> 00:19:15.453
Things that many English speakers would
not be able to use.

00:19:15.653 --> 00:19:18.997
One I noted down was draining a social battery.

00:19:19.080 --> 00:19:20.277
I really like that.

00:19:20.360 --> 00:19:22.280
That was quite poetic.

00:19:22.440 --> 00:19:28.877
The reason why that is so good is that you
used fewer words to explain

00:19:28.960 --> 00:19:30.480
something quite complicated.

00:19:30.563 --> 00:19:33.517
Draining of a social battery,
what does that actually mean?

00:19:33.600 --> 00:19:37.397
It means that you are maybe an introverted
person, that when you're around

00:19:37.480 --> 00:19:39.517
other people, you have less energy.

00:19:39.600 --> 00:19:42.280
You like to be alone and that boosts
your energy a little bit.

00:19:42.560 --> 00:19:45.080
That took me, what,
three or four sentences to explain that,

00:19:45.240 --> 00:19:48.520
or I can just say something like,
it drains my social battery.

00:19:48.720 --> 00:19:53.997
So it is very precise and very economical
and elegant, and you had lots

00:19:54.080 --> 00:19:55.600
of examples of that like this.

00:19:56.280 --> 00:19:59.240
For vocabulary, you would
definitely get a Band 9.

00:19:59.360 --> 00:20:03.760
Grammar, you do make
some little, tiny slips like this.

00:20:04.240 --> 00:20:08.080
That is totally normal,
even for native English speakers.

00:20:08.320 --> 00:20:12.357
A lot of people think that if you make one
tiny little grammatical error,

00:20:12.440 --> 00:20:14.920
you're going to get a Band 5 or
a Band 6 or something like that.

00:20:15.003 --> 00:20:18.917
That is not true because even as
an educated native English speaker

00:20:19.000 --> 00:20:22.557
who teaches English for a living,
I make a little grammatical errors.

00:20:22.640 --> 00:20:23.917
Every English speaker does.

00:20:24.000 --> 00:20:26.477
Even English teachers make
little grammatical errors.

00:20:26.560 --> 00:20:29.357
And in the marking criteria,
it actually says for band 9,

00:20:29.440 --> 00:20:31.920
makes slips very infrequently.

00:20:32.280 --> 00:20:33.720
That's exactly you.

00:20:33.840 --> 00:20:37.520
You do make tiny little errors,
but very infrequently.

00:20:37.680 --> 00:20:40.877
So you would get a Band 9 for grammar.
Okay.

00:20:40.960 --> 00:20:44.160
Now, your weakest area is your fluency.

00:20:44.320 --> 00:20:47.477
This is related to the things that we
talked about at the beginning,

00:20:47.560 --> 00:20:50.480
which is being nervous at the start.

00:20:50.800 --> 00:20:55.280
So there were a lot of mms, ahhs, like, kinda

00:20:55.560 --> 00:21:02.637
we would call them audible pauses or fillers.

00:21:02.720 --> 00:21:07.597
Again, even high-level native English
speakers use those little

00:21:07.680 --> 00:21:09.000
words when we're nervous.

00:21:09.240 --> 00:21:12.077
I'm doing it right now.

00:21:12.160 --> 00:21:14.717
So don't worry too much about it.

00:21:14.800 --> 00:21:19.040
But for the purposes of an IELTS test,
the less nervous that you are,

00:21:19.160 --> 00:21:22.077
the better your fluency is going to be.

00:21:22.160 --> 00:21:26.917
Also, you have that issue of when you're
thinking, it slows down the pace

00:21:27.000 --> 00:21:31.160
that you're speaking at, and you also
have more of those audible pauses.

00:21:31.400 --> 00:21:33.920
Nerves can be solved quite
easily through preparation.

00:21:34.520 --> 00:21:37.800
So doing stuff like this with me and me
giving you feedback,

00:21:38.240 --> 00:21:42.440
you're going to be a lot less nervous
going into the real test because you're

00:21:42.880 --> 00:21:45.280
like, I've been there, done that,
I've practiced so much.

00:21:45.840 --> 00:21:47.720
So it's going to be a walk
in the park for you.

00:21:47.803 --> 00:21:54.480
And the other thing is, try less
to perform and more conversation.

00:21:54.840 --> 00:21:55.997
I know it's difficult.

00:21:56.080 --> 00:21:59.477
Not every examiner is someone that you
would consider a friend,

00:21:59.560 --> 00:22:03.000
but for 15 minutes, pretend you're
talking to a friend in a coffee shop.

00:22:03.280 --> 00:22:07.277
Literally picture that friend or
that family member, and they're

00:22:07.360 --> 00:22:08.560
asking you these questions.

00:22:08.643 --> 00:22:11.677
And I think that will really,
really help your fluency.

00:22:11.760 --> 00:22:13.960
Coherence is fine.
You're answering every question.

00:22:14.043 --> 00:22:15.560
You're developing them enough.

00:22:15.760 --> 00:22:18.440
I think examples could
also help with that.

00:22:18.640 --> 00:22:20.400
Just pick really, really simple things.

00:22:20.680 --> 00:22:25.120
There's nothing in the marking criteria
that talks about levels of ideas.

00:22:25.720 --> 00:22:28.120
You don't get extra marks for good ideas.

00:22:28.320 --> 00:22:32.200
You get extra marks for pronunciation,
fluency, grammar, vocabulary.

00:22:32.400 --> 00:22:36.000
So everything that you do,
all the advice that I'm giving you

00:22:36.320 --> 00:22:38.520
is designed to help those things.

00:22:38.800 --> 00:22:41.360
And for someone like you at a very,
very high level,

00:22:41.720 --> 00:22:46.800
it is more important that we simplify
your ideas because

00:22:47.520 --> 00:22:50.240
you're not thinking about English so much.

00:22:50.600 --> 00:22:55.477
You're thinking about how do I give them
the best answer, the best idea,

00:22:55.560 --> 00:22:58.397
and that can actually hinder your fluency.

00:22:58.480 --> 00:22:59.837
So that would be...

00:22:59.920 --> 00:23:02.240
But overall, I think you
would get a Band 9.

00:23:02.520 --> 00:23:05.557
But on a bad day,
let's say you didn't sleep the night

00:23:05.640 --> 00:23:08.397
before and you didn't get a good breakfast
and you're late,

00:23:08.480 --> 00:23:14.757
and then you get a question that you're
not happy with and you try to overperform,

00:23:14.840 --> 00:23:16.637
that could drop you down.

00:23:16.720 --> 00:23:21.040
But I don't think it's going
to happen, but well done.

00:23:21.280 --> 00:23:22.000
Thank you.

